#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157019 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157238 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d20eac1d-b78f-5ac7-a3b7-fc72b15307a2" :knowledge-point/title "Listing the Elements of a Non-Standard Relation"}] :question/id #uuid "682caa5d-ea1c-5701-b53d-c1e7c5419006" :question/math-academy-id "e-14593" :question/problem "Let $S = \\{ 3, 5 \\}.$ Which set is equivalent to the following relation on $S?$\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\in S^2 \\: : \\: y \\not \\mid 3x \\big\\}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A = A^2.$\n\n$$\nR\\subseteq A\\times A\n$$\n\nIn this case, $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y) \\in S^2$ such that $y  \\not \\mid 3x .$\n\nLet's check each element $x \\in S$ to see which elements $y \\in S$ it could be paired with to satisfy the conditions of our relation.\n\n- If $x=3,$ then the value $y=5$ satisfies $y  \\not\\mid 3x.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(3,5).$\n\n- If $x=5,$  then there is no value of  $y \\in B$ that satisfies $y  \\not\\mid 3x.$\n\nTherefore, we can describe our relation as the following set of ordered pairs:\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (3,5) \\big\\}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157020 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157235 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "481d9abf-1edd-5049-bae7-2e727fe3954c" :knowledge-point/title "Listing the Elements of a Standard Relation"}] :question/id #uuid "40a594ed-35ce-52ed-817e-04398ad06b94" :question/math-academy-id "e-14596" :question/problem "Let $A=\\{1, 2\\}$ and consider the relation \"is greater than or equal to\" $(\\geq)$ on $A.$ If this relation is given by the set $R,$ express $R$ as a set of ordered pairs." :question/worked-solution "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A = A^2.$\n\n$$\nR\\subseteq A^2\n$$\n\nIn this case, $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y) \\in  A^2$ such that $x\\geq y.$\n\nLet's check each element $x \\in A$ to see which elements $y \\in A$ it could be paired with to satisfy the relation.\n\n- If $x=1,$ then the value $y=1$ satisfies $x\\geq y.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(1,1).$\n\n- If $x=2,$ then the values $y=1$ and $y=2$ satisfy $x\\geq y.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(2,1)$ and $(2,2).$\n\nTherefore, we can describe our relation as the following set of ordered pairs:\n\n$$\nR = \\{ (1,1), (2, 1), ( 2, 2) \\}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157021 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157236 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f77177ee-74b0-5681-92a8-ed6994ab9f9c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Elements of Standard Relations"}] :question/id #uuid "aea9d065-3d1c-5b65-81ed-30682fc2b528" :question/math-academy-id "e-14629" :question/problem "Let $A=\\{-2, 0, 2\\}$ and consider the relation \"are congruent modulo $4$\" on $A.$ Write out $R$ as a set of ordered pairs." :question/worked-solution "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A = A^2.$\n\n$$\nR\\subseteq A^2\n$$\n\nIn this case, $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y) \\in A^2$ such that $x \\equiv y \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:4).$\n\nLet's check each element $x \\in A$ to see which elements $y \\in A$ it could be paired with to satisfy the relation.\n\n- If $x=-2,$ then the values $y=-2$ and $y=2$ satisfy $x \\equiv y  \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:4),$ since $4 \\mid (-2+2)$ and $4 \\mid (-2-2).$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(-2,-2)$ and $(-2,2).$\n\n- If $x=0,$ then the value $y=0$ satisfies $x \\equiv y  \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:4),$ since $4 \\mid (0-0).$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(0,0).$\n\n- If $x=2,$ then the values $y=-2$ and $y=2$ satisfy $x \\equiv y  \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:4),$ since $4 \\mid (2+2)$ and $4 \\mid (2-2).$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(2,-2)$ and $(2,2).$\n\nTherefore, we can describe our relation as the following set of ordered pairs:\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (-2,-2), (-2,2), (0,0), (2,-2), (2,2)\\big\\}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157022 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157237 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8479a60b-b5db-5ad4-8100-fd2db8002091" :knowledge-point/title "Using the x R y Notation"}] :question/id #uuid "0b2b99c2-a0fc-5194-9139-4348ef53d50a" :question/math-academy-id "e-14633" :question/problem "Consider the following set:\n\n$$\nA = \\big\\{0, 1\\big\\}\n$$\n\nGiven the relation $R$ on $A$ defined by\n\n$$\nx\\:R\\: y \\quad \\Leftrightarrow \\quad x\\neq y,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $0\\: R\\: 0$\n\n- $0 \\: R\\: 1$\n\n- $1 \\: \\cancel{\\!R\\!}\\: 1$" :question/worked-solution "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A = A^2.$\n\n$$\nR\\subseteq A^2\n$$\n\nWe also have the following notations:\n\n- $x \\: R \\: y$ if and only if $(x,y) \\in R$\n\n- $x \\: \\cancel{\\!R\\!} \\: y$ if and only if $(x,y) \\notin R$\n\nIn this case, we have $x \\: R \\: y$ if and only if $x\\neq y.$ For this relation, note that\n\n- the symbols $R$ and $\\neq$ are interchangeable, and\n\n- the symbols $\\cancel{\\!R\\!}$ and $=$ are also interchangeable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $\\neq$, we have $0 \\neq 0,$ which is false.\n\n- Statement II is true. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $\\neq,$ we have $0 \\neq 1,$ which is true.\n\n- Statement III is true. Replacing $\\cancel{\\!R\\!}$ with the symbol $=,$ we have $1 = 1,$ which is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411409 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157235 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "481d9abf-1edd-5049-bae7-2e727fe3954c" :knowledge-point/title "Listing the Elements of a Standard Relation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5de33ec-5eb2-57e5-a79c-a3e57a87a5b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411356}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5de33ec-5eb2-57e5-a79c-a3e57a87a5b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411356} :answer-field/id #uuid "563805de-8b3a-5464-bcca-999500e33b1c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411387} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "77e616b4-72e5-5db8-b77f-08c2c3921e34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411329}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77e616b4-72e5-5db8-b77f-08c2c3921e34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411329} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fc40920-1ae1-5234-8dd8-1a5fb656854a" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411392} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "755a51b0-9b5d-5c66-b02d-25d90741165f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411327}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "755a51b0-9b5d-5c66-b02d-25d90741165f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411327} :answer-field/id #uuid "d86a9c9b-e486-55a1-b0ac-a61fced21ce5" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411404}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0d410e49-d4f5-55b7-a3f6-063935c55bfd" :question/math-academy-id "q-212073" :question/problem "Let $A={1,2,3}$ and consider the relation \"is smaller than\" $(<)$ on $A.$ If this relation is given by the set $R,$ express $R$ as a set of ordered pairs.\n\n$R={(1,2),(1,$\n{{field-1}}$),($\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}$)}$" :question/worked-solution "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆{A}^{2}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in {A}^{2}$ such that $x<y.$\n\nLet's check each element $x\\in A$ to see which elements $y\\in A$ it could be paired with to satisfy the relation.\n\n- If $x=1,$ then the values $y=2$ and $y=3$ satisfy $x<y.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(1,2),(1,3).$\n\n- If $x=2,$ then the value $y=3$ satisfies $x<y.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(2,3).$\n\n- If $x=3,$ then there is no value of  $y\\in A$ that satisfies $x<y.$\n\nTherefore, we can describe our relation as the following set of ordered pairs:\n\n$$\nR={(1,2),(1,3),(2,3)}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411427 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157237 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8479a60b-b5db-5ad4-8100-fd2db8002091" :knowledge-point/title "Using the x R y Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03cf3dd2-1d1e-571a-b1f1-4ac972be63d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411287} {:answer/id #uuid "256131e7-1b92-545f-8144-d6cc2b71dbde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411302} {:answer/id #uuid "744c2bf9-2be2-5a86-9e71-395056b3825f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411326} {:answer/id #uuid "f21c9568-89f5-550c-aeca-3135fd1e4015" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411372} {:answer/id #uuid "fd3906e4-2bc8-51c1-b715-a02b38473796" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411381}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd3906e4-2bc8-51c1-b715-a02b38473796" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411381} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9c640c9-599a-5e63-a2a4-02ba3cb94fbb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411394}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fbdd486a-767d-532e-973f-5e22a4525b04" :question/math-academy-id "q-212638" :question/problem "Consider the following set:\n\n$$\nA={e,\\pi }\n$$\n\nGiven the relation $R$ on $A$ defined by\n\n$$\nx\\,R\\,y\\,⇔\\,x\\ne y,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $e\\,R\\,e$\n- $e\\,R\\,\\pi $\n- $\\pi \\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,\\pi $" :question/worked-solution "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆{A}^{2}\n$$\n\nWe also have the following notations:\n\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\in R$\n\n- $x\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\notin R$\n\nIn this case, we have $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $x\\ne y.$ For this relation, note that\n\n- the symbols $R$ and $\\ne $ are interchangeable, and\n\n- the symbols $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ and $=$ are also interchangeable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $\\ne $, we have $e\\ne e,$ which is false.\n\n- Statement II is true. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $\\ne ,$ we have $e\\ne \\pi ,$ which is true.\n\n- Statement III is true. Replacing $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ with the symbol $=,$ we have $\\pi =\\pi ,$ which is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411413 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157235 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "481d9abf-1edd-5049-bae7-2e727fe3954c" :knowledge-point/title "Listing the Elements of a Standard Relation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2dd04c2b-bbc5-5101-8257-ac99a88366e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411307}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2dd04c2b-bbc5-5101-8257-ac99a88366e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411307} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb89d379-fa2f-5a85-96b3-2cc4ced981d4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411401} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7bffe33b-68b3-5c77-8718-5f29ce5b2417" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411332}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7bffe33b-68b3-5c77-8718-5f29ce5b2417" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411332} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4e45220-d597-524c-83e5-31b20d817060" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411405} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1051535d-950d-5394-b25e-445ffff8656a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411296}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1051535d-950d-5394-b25e-445ffff8656a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411296} :answer-field/id #uuid "f061e5ea-3298-55a4-9377-e3f6be2f4109" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411406}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3d53ba03-d2db-5ca1-93a3-c5b7fe5ecc36" :question/math-academy-id "q-212662" :question/problem "Let $A={0,4,5}$ and consider the relation \"is equal to\" $(=)$ on $A.$  If this relation is given by the set $R,$ express $R$ as a set of ordered pairs.\n\n$R={(0,0),(4,$\n{{field-1}}$),($\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}$)}$" :question/worked-solution "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆{A}^{2}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in {A}^{2}$ such that $x=y.$\n\nLet's check each element $x\\in A$ to see which elements $y\\in A$ it could be paired with to satisfy the relation.\n\n- If $x=0,$ then the value $y=0$ satisfies $x=y.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(0,0).$\n\n- If $x=4,$ then the value $y=4$ satisfies $x=y.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(4,4).$\n\n- If $x=5,$ then the value $y=5$ satisfies $x=y.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(5,5).$\n\nTherefore, we can describe our relation as the following set of ordered pairs:\n\n$$\nR={(0,0),(4,4),(5,5)}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411411 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157235 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "481d9abf-1edd-5049-bae7-2e727fe3954c" :knowledge-point/title "Listing the Elements of a Standard Relation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0779fdc3-adde-5db9-bd2f-058c400a1fe9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(-1,1),(1,-1),(1,1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411293} {:answer/id #uuid "66a180fd-4f9d-5162-86fd-4bcd2b980abe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(-1,-1),(1,1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411322} {:answer/id #uuid "7ff5ecee-cd3a-535f-b7a3-800070bca446" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(-1,-1),(-1,1),(1,-1),(1,1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411333} {:answer/id #uuid "a528ef20-f3df-5a73-abfe-b6f48641ed44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(-1,1),(1,-1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411345} {:answer/id #uuid "c0fb0e19-1e92-54d0-8a47-e9675c5fc5d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(-1,1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411355}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a528ef20-f3df-5a73-abfe-b6f48641ed44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(-1,1),(1,-1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411345} :answer-field/id #uuid "5bce2b8a-2a35-542a-8d39-426bc0f65991" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411388}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "27a0c14e-ebcc-5c2f-8221-848aff0bd39b" :question/math-academy-id "q-212668" :question/problem "Let $A={-1,1}$ and consider the relation \"is not equal to\" $(\\ne )$ on $A.$ Which of the following sets describes the relation?" :question/worked-solution "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆{A}^{2}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in {A}^{2}$ such that $x\\ne y.$\n\nLet's check each element $x\\in A$ to see which elements $y\\in A$ it could be paired with to satisfy the relation.\n\n- If $x=-1,$ then the value $y=1$ satisfies $x\\ne y.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(-1,1).$\n\n- If $x=1,$ then the value $y=-1$ satisfies $x\\ne y.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(1,-1).$\n\nTherefore, we can describe our relation as the following set of ordered pairs:\n\n$$\nR={(-1,1),(1,-1)}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411419 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 1759